    {
      "id": "02_spectrum_and_mask",
      "title": "Spectrum and threshold decision",
      "caption": "These are canonical pre-mask FFT bins from the C++ model, not an FFT of the entire output stream. An eligible bin is suppressed when magnitude squared is strictly less than THR2. The lower panel shows the actual final mask. Zero magnitudes use a display floor of 1 on the log plot."
    },
    {
      "id": "03_threshold_waveforms_and_error",
      "title": "Threshold impact on waveforms",
      "caption": "Increasing THR2 changes the output and can spread error around the finite input boundaries. Shaded areas in the lower panel lie outside the delayed input support. They remain part of the error calculation."
    },
    {
      "id": "04_threshold_tradeoff",
      "title": "Threshold tradeoff",
      "caption": "At THR2=10^11, this vector suppresses 93.06% of eligible unique-bin instances and retains 99.21% of weighted eligible spectral magnitude squared, with 22.71 LSB full-stream RMSE. Spectral retention is a representation metric, not electrical energy saved."
    },
    {
      "id": "05_spectrum_and_mask_heatmap",
      "title": "Spectrum and mask across frames",
      "caption": "The upper heatmap uses canonical pre-mask magnitudes with a log-display floor of 1. The lower heatmap shows exact binary mask decisions. Protected DC at k=0 stays kept. Frame index is not a hardware-clock or measured-time axis."
    },
    {
      "id": "06_window_and_overlap",
      "title": "Window and overlap-add coefficients",
      "caption": "The top plot decodes the frozen coefficient table. The lower plot shows the quantized squared-window overlap sum, which is close to but not mathematically identical to one. This coefficient property supports reconstruction but is not a standalone proof for all signals."
    },
